 Environment Variables in Images
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Use ENV and ARG instructions in Containerfiles to manage configuration
Define environment variables in container images
Override environment variables at runtime
Inspect environment variables in running containers
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line knowledge
Podman or Docker installed (Podman recommended for OpenShift alignment)
Text editor (Vim, Nano, or VS Code)
Internet access to pull base images
Setup Requirements
Ensure Podman is installed:
sudo dnf install podman  # For RHEL/Fedora
Verify installation:
podman --version
Task 1: Define Environment Variables in Containerfile
Subtask 1.1: Create a Basic Containerfile
Create a new directory for the lab:
mkdir env-lab && cd env-lab
Create a Containerfile:
cat <<EOF > Containerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal
ENV APP_NAME="MyApp" \
    APP_VERSION="1.0" \
    APP_ENV="development"
CMD echo "Running \$APP_NAME v\$APP_VERSION in \$APP_ENV mode"
EOF
Subtask 1.2: Build and Run the Image
Build the image:
podman build -t env-demo .
Run the container:
podman run env-demo
Expected Output:
Running MyApp v1.0 in development mode
Task 2: Override Environment Variables at Runtime
Subtask 2.1: Override Using Command Line
Run the container with overridden variables:
podman run -e APP_ENV="production" -e APP_VERSION="2.0" env-demo
Expected Output:
Running MyApp v2.0 in production mode
Subtask 2.2: Use Environment Files
Create an environment file:
cat <<EOF > app.env
APP_NAME=ProductionApp
APP_VERSION=3.0
APP_ENV=staging
EOF
Run with the environment file:
podman run --env-file=app.env env-demo
Expected Output:
Running ProductionApp v3.0 in staging mode
Task 3: Inspect Variables in Running Containers
Subtask 3.1: View Environment Variables
Run the container in detached mode:
podman run -d --name env-container env-demo
Inspect the environment variables:
podman exec env-container env
Expected Output (partial):
APP_NAME=MyApp
APP_VERSION=1.0
APP_ENV=development
Subtask 3.2: Use ARG for Build-Time Variables
Modify the Containerfile:
cat <<EOF > Containerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal
ARG APP_BUILD_NUMBER
ENV APP_BUILD=\$APP_BUILD_NUMBER
CMD echo "Build number: \$APP_BUILD"
EOF
Build with build argument:
podman build --build-arg APP_BUILD_NUMBER=42 -t arg-demo .
Run the container:
podman run arg-demo
Expected Output:
Build number: 42
Troubleshooting Tips
If variables don't appear: Verify correct syntax (no spaces around = in ENV)
For ARG issues: Ensure build arguments are passed with --build-arg
Permission errors: Run Podman with sudo if needed (not recommended for production)
Conclusion
In this lab, you've learned to:

Define persistent environment variables using ENV in Containerfiles
Override environment variables at runtime using -e and --env-file
Inspect environment variables in running containers
Use build-time arguments with ARG for flexible image builds
These skills are essential for configuring containerized applications in OpenShift and other Kubernetes environments.

